-- title:
--   Abel's theorem, necessity: principal divisors have lattice periods
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, and assume (`hfg`) that there is a transcendental $x \in F$ over $\mathbb{C}$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\mathbb{C}(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume `IsCurveOver ℂ F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ has an associated divisor on the set of places $\mathrm{Place}(\mathbb{C},F)$ — valuation subrings of $F$ containing the image of $\mathbb{C}$, proper, with principal ideals — whose value at $v$ is $\mathrm{ord}_v f = -\log v(f)$ and whose degree is $0$; each residue field is finite over $\mathbb{C}$; and $\Omega_{F/\mathbb{C}}$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. Assume `HasCanonicalDivisor`: every nonzero $\omega \in \Omega_{F/\mathbb{C}}$ has a divisor with values $v \mapsto \mathrm{ord}_v \omega$. Assume $\mathrm{Place}(\mathbb{C},F)$ carries a topology and an analytic complex charted-manifold structure modelled on $\mathbb{C}$, and is compact, Hausdorff and connected, and (`hF`) that for every $f \neq 0$ and every place $v$ the function $z \mapsto \mathrm{evalAt}_{\varphi_v^{-1}(z)}(f)$ is meromorphic at $\varphi_v(v)$ with meromorphic order exactly $\mathrm{ord}_v f$, where $\varphi_v$ is the extended chart at $v$. Then for every $n$, every family $b : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \Omega_{F/\mathbb{C}}$ of differentials each of which is, at every place $v$, of the form $f \cdot \mathrm{dCoord}_v$ with $f$ in the valuation subring of $v$, every base place $P_0$, and every divisor $D$ that is principal, i.e. $D(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v f$ for all $v$ for some $f \neq 0$, the vector $\mathrm{abelJacobiDiv}(b, P_0)(D) = \sum_v D(v) \bigl(\int_{\gamma_v} b_i\bigr)_i$, the additive extension to divisors of the path-integral vector along a chosen path from $P_0$ to $v$, lies in the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{C}^n$ spanned by the period vectors $\bigl(\int_\gamma b_i\bigr)_i$ of loops $\gamma$ at arbitrary places.
--
--   This is the necessity half of Abel's theorem for a compact Riemann surface: the Abel–Jacobi map kills principal divisors, so it descends to divisor classes. It is used in the identification of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ with a quotient of $\mathbb{C}^n$ by the period lattice and in the comparison of correspondences with integral matrices on the Tate module.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_abelJacobiDiv_mem_pathPeriodLattice_of_isPrincipal.lean

